00:43:32.381 --> 00:43:33.862
I would think baked beans are just like,

00:43:33.862 --> 00:43:35.341
why would you ever eat baked beans?

00:43:35.341 --> 00:43:36.647
- Well, 'cause in Australia
we eat baked beans

00:43:36.647 --> 00:43:38.575
quite often as well.

00:43:38.575 --> 00:43:40.327
Sometimes I would eat it on toast,

00:43:40.327 --> 00:43:41.988
sometimes I would just
fucking crack open a can.

00:43:41.988 --> 00:43:43.581
- Yeah, in university,
I basically lived off

00:43:43.581 --> 00:43:45.593
just beans in cans and I
would just heat them up

00:43:45.593 --> 00:43:46.426
and eat them for dinner.

00:43:46.426 --> 00:43:47.877
- That sounds disgusting to me.

00:43:47.877 --> 00:43:49.034
- Oh, yeah, it was.
- I wouldn't be able

00:43:49.034 --> 00:43:49.867
to live off it.

00:43:49.867 --> 00:43:50.736
- I didn't know how to cook,

00:43:50.736 --> 00:43:53.847
so I would just boil
pasta and put salt on it

00:43:53.847 --> 00:43:55.367
and eat the pasta.

00:43:55.367 --> 00:43:57.641
- I love how the cooking
experience at university

00:43:57.641 --> 00:44:00.835
is just how many ways can I cook pasta?

00:44:00.835 --> 00:44:01.773
It's just that.

00:44:01.773 --> 00:44:03.429
- It took me a year to figure out

00:44:03.429 --> 00:44:05.396
that you could just pour the pot sauces

00:44:05.396 --> 00:44:06.229
on top of the pasta.

00:44:06.229 --> 00:44:07.362
(laughing)

00:44:07.362 --> 00:44:10.348
Because I was so content and
I didn't wanna look stupid

00:44:10.348 --> 00:44:12.205
in front of my uni mates,

00:44:12.205 --> 00:44:14.447
and I remember my mum offered
to teach me how to cook

00:44:14.447 --> 00:44:15.941
and I was like, "I don't need that.

00:44:15.941 --> 00:44:16.774
How hard can it be?"

00:44:16.774 --> 00:44:18.115
- I got this.
- How hard can it be?

00:44:18.115 --> 00:44:21.310
So yeah, I was eating plain
pasta and beans for a year.

00:44:21.310 --> 00:44:22.499
- Yeah.
- It's pretty depressing.

00:44:22.499 --> 00:44:23.814
- Like cup ramen, cup ramen-

00:44:23.814 --> 00:44:26.356
- Oh, yeah, cup, the first
day of university I went

00:44:26.356 --> 00:44:29.495
and bought a crate of pot
noodles and lived off them.

00:44:29.495 --> 00:44:30.515
That was beautiful.

00:44:30.515 --> 00:44:32.224
- You can't go wrong
with pot noodles, dude.

00:44:32.224 --> 00:44:33.373
- We should probably get
back onto Japanese food,

00:44:33.373 --> 00:44:34.456
shouldn't we?

00:44:34.456 --> 00:44:36.510
- Well, going back to Japanese,

00:44:36.510 --> 00:44:38.174
recently, funnily enough,

00:44:38.174 --> 00:44:42.469
I just went to the cup ramen
museum in Yokohama this week,

00:44:42.469 --> 00:44:45.070
which, the only reason we
went to the cup ramen museum

00:44:45.070 --> 00:44:45.903
was just because-

00:44:45.903 --> 00:44:46.944
- It's just there.
- It was just there.

00:44:46.944 --> 00:44:49.005
I was like, whatever,
it's something to do.

00:44:49.005 --> 00:44:51.614
But it was actually really
interesting and really fun.

00:44:51.614 --> 00:44:54.631
It was not someplace I thought to go

00:44:54.631 --> 00:44:56.860
that would be a fun, exciting place to go,

00:44:56.860 --> 00:45:00.193
but it was fun to learn about cup ramen.

00:45:01.081 --> 00:45:03.289
I had no idea that basically,

00:45:03.289 --> 00:45:06.939
cup ramen exists because of Americans.

00:45:06.939 --> 00:45:09.856
Because instant noodle was invented

00:45:11.054 --> 00:45:13.659
in the usual packeted way,

00:45:13.659 --> 00:45:15.498
and then they tried to sell it to America.

00:45:15.498 --> 00:45:17.508
And Americans were just
like, "How do we eat this?"

00:45:17.508 --> 00:45:19.545
Well, you're gonna have
to use a fucking chopstick

00:45:19.545 --> 00:45:22.239
with bowls, like, that's too inefficient.

00:45:22.239 --> 00:45:24.747
So they just started putting
it in their soda cans

00:45:24.747 --> 00:45:27.716
or no, not the soda cans, the soda cups,

00:45:27.716 --> 00:45:31.633
and just put the ramen
in and put hot water in.

00:45:32.785 --> 00:45:34.502
And that's how cup ramen was invented!

00:45:34.502 --> 00:45:35.585
- Oh wow.

00:45:35.585 --> 00:45:37.521
So cup ramen is an American invention?

00:45:37.521 --> 00:45:38.400
- Yeah, well it's-

00:45:38.400 --> 00:45:40.582
- It was invented in Japan,
the method of doing it

00:45:40.582 --> 00:45:41.415
with the noodles-

00:45:41.415 --> 00:45:42.579
- Yeah, but it was when they tried

00:45:42.579 --> 00:45:45.672
to sell it to Americans that
he saw Americans doing that,

00:45:45.672 --> 00:45:47.956
and he was like, "Let's just
put it in the fucking cup."

00:45:47.956 --> 00:45:51.031
So, thank you, America, for cup ramen.

00:45:51.031 --> 00:45:52.114
- Yeah, and if you're in prison,

00:45:52.114 --> 00:45:53.870
it's your currency.

00:45:53.870 --> 00:45:55.466
- Cup ramen currency.
- So you don't want Vox video,

00:45:55.466 --> 00:45:57.990
it's actually currency in
most prisons, that's so funny.

00:45:57.990 --> 00:46:00.208
- But yeah, in terms of Japanese food,

00:46:00.208 --> 00:46:04.249
what are some Japanese
food you want more people

00:46:04.249 --> 00:46:06.303
to know about or that you have discovered

00:46:06.303 --> 00:46:09.521
since moving to Japan that maybe
not many people know about?

00:46:09.521 --> 00:46:10.433
- Well, you know my answer, right?

00:46:10.433 --> 00:46:11.941
- I already know your answer,

00:46:11.941 --> 00:46:12.885
we've talked about this before.

00:46:12.885 --> 00:46:13.885
Go on, Joey.

00:46:15.151 --> 00:46:16.754
Simp for your dish.

00:46:16.754 --> 00:46:19.604
- I'm simping for a little
dish called tsukemen.

00:46:19.604 --> 00:46:21.442
It's not ramen, it's tsukemen.

00:46:21.442 --> 00:46:24.528
So basically, tsukemen, oversimplified.

00:46:24.528 --> 00:46:28.694
Think of ramen but separate
the noodles from the broth

00:46:28.694 --> 00:46:31.126
and make that broth thick as a bitch.

00:46:31.126 --> 00:46:32.368
- I will say when I first-

00:46:32.368 --> 00:46:35.403
(laughing)

00:46:35.403 --> 00:46:38.251
When I was first, I saw it
on the menu, I was like,

00:46:38.251 --> 00:46:39.915
"Why the fuck would I want my noodles

00:46:39.915 --> 00:46:41.066
separated from the broth?"

00:46:41.066 --> 00:46:43.524
That sounds like it's
demarinating the noodles.

00:46:43.524 --> 00:46:44.948
- Yeah, an extra step.

00:46:44.948 --> 00:46:46.067
- Yeah, it sounded like it was just

00:46:46.067 --> 00:46:47.694
making the noodles less flavorful.

00:46:47.694 --> 00:46:49.668
- It just didn't make any logical sense.

00:46:49.668 --> 00:46:52.883
- But somehow, it makes the
noodles even more flavorful

00:46:52.883 --> 00:46:54.436
when you dip them into
this and you basically

00:46:54.436 --> 00:46:56.119
have the broth on the noodles (mumbling).

00:46:56.119 --> 00:46:58.022
- Because the thing with ramen broth

00:46:58.022 --> 00:47:00.273
is that it's very liquidy
because the noodle

00:47:00.273 --> 00:47:02.029
- It's so diluted.
- is gonna be sitting

00:47:02.029 --> 00:47:04.143
in this broth for a long, long time.

00:47:04.143 --> 00:47:04.976
- And you can drink it.

00:47:04.976 --> 00:47:06.103
- And you can drink it.

00:47:06.103 --> 00:47:09.107
But tsukemen broths, they
have to make it thick,

00:47:09.107 --> 00:47:11.264
because when you put the noodles in

00:47:11.264 --> 00:47:13.053
and then you take it
out again, as you said,

00:47:13.053 --> 00:47:15.235
the broth has to stick to the noodles.

00:47:15.235 --> 00:47:16.068
And that shit is,

00:47:16.068 --> 00:47:18.575
the flavor of that is so fucking powerful.

00:47:18.575 --> 00:47:21.023
- And then when you're
done eating the noodles

00:47:21.023 --> 00:47:24.620
you can pour a soup into
the broth to dilute it

00:47:24.620 --> 00:47:25.453
to make it drinkable.

00:47:25.453 --> 00:47:27.411
- It's basically like concentrated ramen.

00:47:27.411 --> 00:47:29.937
That's basically the first time,

00:47:29.937 --> 00:47:33.390
but I don't know, because I
feel no matter what we say

00:47:33.390 --> 00:47:35.360
or how much we try to sell it,

00:47:35.360 --> 00:47:36.941
you can't sell it in words.

00:47:36.941 --> 00:47:39.755
- Because it just doesn't
look as pretty as ramen.

00:47:39.755 --> 00:47:41.043
Ramen is so pretty.

00:47:41.043 --> 00:47:43.494
- And you explain it logically,
and you're just like,

00:47:43.494 --> 00:47:45.563
that just sounds like
ramen with extra steps.

00:47:45.563 --> 00:47:47.501
What makes it so amazing?
- That's exactly what you guys

00:47:47.501 --> 00:47:49.342
said when I tried to sell it
to you guys, and I was like,

00:47:49.342 --> 00:47:52.920
"Trust me, let's go to one of
my favorite tsukemen places

00:47:52.920 --> 00:47:55.389
in Ikebukuro and you can
just eat it and tell me."

00:47:55.389 --> 00:47:56.472
And I took them-

00:47:56.472 --> 00:47:59.041
- And I remember we were chatting
the biggest, maddest shit.

00:47:59.041 --> 00:48:01.832
We were like, "Sure Joey, sure, whatever."

00:48:01.832 --> 00:48:04.034
And then we got the dish
and we just sat there

00:48:04.034 --> 00:48:06.504
in silence for a good 10 minutes

00:48:06.504 --> 00:48:08.336
as we were just eating this.

00:48:08.336 --> 00:48:12.377
And I remember the second
place you took me to,

00:48:12.377 --> 00:48:16.815
I swear, I had an existential
crisis eating the tsukemen.

00:48:16.815 --> 00:48:18.113
I remember you both were eating,

00:48:18.113 --> 00:48:19.283
you were just staring at me,

00:48:19.283 --> 00:48:21.069
and I was just looking at his bowl of,

00:48:21.069 --> 00:48:23.842
I was just eating this bowl
of tsukemen being just like...

00:48:23.842 --> 00:48:25.016
- [Connor] It's perfect.

00:48:25.016 --> 00:48:26.210
- It's perfect.

00:48:26.210 --> 00:48:27.119
I'll tell you what it felt like,

00:48:27.119 --> 00:48:29.228
it felt like that meme
in "Ratatouille" where,

00:48:29.228 --> 00:48:31.175
(laughing)

00:48:31.175 --> 00:48:34.109
where I had one bite
and it just took me back

00:48:34.109 --> 00:48:35.845
to my childhood or something like that.

00:48:35.845 --> 00:48:40.160
I was having a flashback to
this dish grandma made me.

00:48:40.160 --> 00:48:41.903
My grandma's never made me tsukemen,

00:48:41.903 --> 00:48:45.407
but it gave me that same homely feeling

00:48:45.407 --> 00:48:49.277
that I had when eating a home
cooked meal, back as a kid.

00:48:49.277 --> 00:48:51.560
And I was like, "What
is happening right now?"

00:48:51.560 --> 00:48:53.553
- I've never seen anyone
have a "Food Wars" moment

00:48:53.553 --> 00:48:55.567
in real life, but that was legitimately

00:48:55.567 --> 00:48:57.179
the closest I've ever seen to-

00:48:57.179 --> 00:48:59.144
- I was this close to fucking crying.

00:48:59.144 --> 00:49:00.836
(laughing)

00:49:00.836 --> 00:49:02.498
- I remember I stopped to,

00:49:02.498 --> 00:49:04.059
'cause you were sitting
opposite me and I was eating,

00:49:04.059 --> 00:49:06.197
and I looked over and you were kinda just

00:49:06.197 --> 00:49:07.616
silently staring at it.

00:49:07.616 --> 00:49:09.135
And I remember I said to him, like,

00:49:09.135 --> 00:49:11.131
"Are your eyes watering?

00:49:11.131 --> 00:49:12.781
Are you really about to cry over

00:49:12.781 --> 00:49:14.163
this bowl of tsukemen?"

00:49:14.163 --> 00:49:15.152
(laughing)

00:49:15.152 --> 00:49:17.345
- That, that is uh, that definitely did.

00:49:17.345 --> 00:49:19.052
- Meanwhile, I was just there in my mind

00:49:19.052 --> 00:49:21.729
like fucking Tyler Wall, like "Yes!"

00:49:21.729 --> 00:49:23.062
Just fucking eating it.

00:49:23.062 --> 00:49:24.820
- And then I remember the next day,

00:49:24.820 --> 00:49:27.081
we were talking about whatever the fuck

00:49:27.081 --> 00:49:29.612
we were talking about,
and Garnt just texted,

00:49:29.612 --> 00:49:30.812
fully serious, being like,

00:49:30.812 --> 00:49:33.359
"I had a dream about
that tsukemen last night.

00:49:33.359 --> 00:49:35.290
I actually had a dream
about that tsukemen,

00:49:35.290 --> 00:49:36.589
because it's that fucking good."

00:49:36.589 --> 00:49:38.815
- As well because they have the meat

00:49:38.815 --> 00:49:40.314
and all the veg in the broth,

00:49:40.314 --> 00:49:41.911
when you bite into the veg or the meat,

00:49:41.911 --> 00:49:45.044
they've absorbed this intense
broth and it just bursts,

00:49:45.044 --> 00:49:47.474
and it's so fucking good.
- And the meat is so soft

00:49:47.474 --> 00:49:48.975
and just breaks apart.
- Yeah, it's so tender,

00:49:48.975 --> 00:49:49.990
so tender.

00:49:49.990 --> 00:49:52.266
- Honestly, I'm not
gonna say that the ramen

00:49:52.266 --> 00:49:54.045
in Japan is bad or anything,

00:49:54.045 --> 00:49:56.320
there's some fucking
fantastic ramen out there.

00:49:56.320 --> 00:49:59.203
But I just feel that
tsukemen is such an underdog

00:49:59.203 --> 00:50:00.934
because people just, as you say,

00:50:00.934 --> 00:50:03.769
people just think that it's
just an inferior ramen.

00:50:03.769 --> 00:50:04.766
- Yeah, I mean, that's what I thought

00:50:04.766 --> 00:50:07.417
and I didn't even know about
tsukemen until I moved here

00:50:07.417 --> 00:50:08.645
and you told me about it.

00:50:08.645 --> 00:50:10.941
And I feel like people only come here

00:50:10.941 --> 00:50:12.604
and they only know ramen.

00:50:12.604 --> 00:50:15.312
Because when I came here,
I only knew ramen as well.

00:50:15.312 --> 00:50:17.023
- I totally don't blame
people who are like

00:50:17.023 --> 00:50:18.248
"Oh yeah, I just wanna eat sushi

00:50:18.248 --> 00:50:19.394
and I just wanna eat ramen."

00:50:19.394 --> 00:50:21.802
But it just frustrates me when there's,

00:50:21.802 --> 00:50:23.635
you go to a ramen shop,
and they usually have

00:50:23.635 --> 00:50:25.264
tsukemen on the menu as well.

00:50:25.264 --> 00:50:26.641
And yet they just completely ignore that

00:50:26.641 --> 00:50:29.076
and just go for the basic veg ramen.

00:50:29.076 --> 00:50:32.838
- Yeah, and I feel like you do
get a better ramen experience

00:50:32.838 --> 00:50:35.145
here than you do anywhere
else in the world.

00:50:35.145 --> 00:50:36.700
- It just frustrates me
when people are like,

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:38.614
"I'm gonna go to Ichiran Ramen",

00:50:38.614 --> 00:50:40.898
and it's like, you can
have an Ichiran in America!

00:50:40.898 --> 00:50:42.406
Why did you fly all the way to Japan

00:50:42.406 --> 00:50:43.673
to have something you can have in America?

00:50:43.673 --> 00:50:46.804
- I do not understand
why people come to Japan

00:50:46.804 --> 00:50:49.729
and they try, what's the curry place?

00:50:49.729 --> 00:50:51.890
- CoCo curry.
- Is it CoCo curry that's

00:50:51.890 --> 00:50:52.973
like the international one?

00:50:52.973 --> 00:50:54.757
- Yeah, yeah.
- Yeah, CoCo Ichiban.

00:50:54.757 --> 00:50:55.590
- I don't know why they come here

00:50:55.590 --> 00:50:57.835
and just try the CoCo curry here.

00:50:57.835 --> 00:50:59.041
- In defense of both those chains,

00:50:59.041 --> 00:51:00.564
I'll be devil's advocate,

00:51:00.564 --> 00:51:03.857
I went to Ichiran Ramen maybe a month ago.

00:51:03.857 --> 00:51:05.230
I thought it was pretty fucking good.

00:51:05.230 --> 00:51:06.220
I thought it was like-

00:51:06.220 --> 00:51:07.936
- It's not bad.

00:51:07.936 --> 00:51:08.981
It's just mid.

00:51:08.981 --> 00:51:11.129
(laughing)

00:51:11.129 --> 00:51:11.962
It is.

00:51:11.962 --> 00:51:12.795
- If you don't live here, right?

00:51:12.795 --> 00:51:14.029
And you don't wanna fuck around

00:51:14.029 --> 00:51:16.376
with going down these alleyways,
looking on Google Maps

00:51:16.376 --> 00:51:18.575
trying to find shit, I can
understand where you're like,

00:51:18.575 --> 00:51:21.066
"I want a good ramen that delivers,

00:51:21.066 --> 00:51:22.733
and I don't have to talk to anyone.

00:51:22.733 --> 00:51:23.651
They have an English menu."

00:51:23.651 --> 00:51:26.183
I get that, 'cause sometimes
these ramen places, bro,

00:51:26.183 --> 00:51:28.297
no pictures, only Kanji.

00:51:28.297 --> 00:51:30.251
You've gotta give your ticket to the guy,

00:51:30.251 --> 00:51:31.953
you've gotta hope that he calls you out

00:51:31.953 --> 00:51:33.232
and you know that it's you, right?

00:51:33.232 --> 00:51:34.787
It's a little, you know, you know-

00:51:34.787 --> 00:51:36.035
- You've gotta figure
out if he's asking you

00:51:36.035 --> 00:51:37.853
if you want extra veg or whatever.

00:51:37.853 --> 00:51:40.632
- 'Cause you hand it to
him and he'll ask what size

00:51:40.632 --> 00:51:41.778
and you'll be like, what?

00:51:41.778 --> 00:51:42.910
(laughing)

00:51:42.910 --> 00:51:44.155
Or, do you want rice?

00:51:44.155 --> 00:51:45.798
Rice they ask some and sometimes,

00:51:45.798 --> 00:51:48.511
and 'cause the word for rice
and meal is the same, gohan-

00:51:48.511 --> 00:51:49.661
- Gohan.
- And so one time

00:51:49.661 --> 00:51:50.576
they asked me "Gohan?",

00:51:50.576 --> 00:51:52.264
I just completely forgot that it was rice.

00:51:52.264 --> 00:51:53.389
And I'm like, "Yeah, I want a meal,

00:51:53.389 --> 00:51:55.891
I just gave you my tickets you dumb fuck.

00:51:55.891 --> 00:51:56.858
Take my ticket!"

00:51:56.858 --> 00:51:58.902
- Yeah, I know that's Goku's son.

00:51:58.902 --> 00:52:00.559
(laughing drowns out speaker)

00:52:00.559 --> 00:52:03.134
- Yeah, so I get it with Ichiran Ramen.

00:52:03.134 --> 00:52:04.080
Okay, it was a little expensive though.

00:52:04.080 --> 00:52:04.913
I'll give you that.

00:52:04.913 --> 00:52:05.746
- It is.

00:52:05.746 --> 00:52:07.767
- The, the noodles were
thin though, it was good.

00:52:07.767 --> 00:52:09.137
I wanna start a series where I go

00:52:09.137 --> 00:52:10.336
around reviewing ramen chains.

00:52:10.336 --> 00:52:11.857
- Yeah, but that's the
problem I have with Ichiran

00:52:11.857 --> 00:52:13.508
is that it is the thin noodles,

00:52:13.508 --> 00:52:15.268
and I don't have a problem
with the thin noodles

00:52:15.268 --> 00:52:17.797
but I like my noodles
like I like my women.

00:52:17.797 --> 00:52:19.022
I like them thick.

00:52:19.022 --> 00:52:20.710
(laughing)

00:52:20.710 --> 00:52:22.821
- I was wondering where
you're going with that.

00:52:22.821 --> 00:52:24.347
- Joey's like the inappropriate uncle

00:52:24.347 --> 00:52:27.392
that has to mention something
sexual every 10 seconds.

00:52:27.392 --> 00:52:28.225
- Of course!

00:52:28.225 --> 00:52:29.972
It's the only way people relate to me.

00:52:29.972 --> 00:52:30.892
(laughing)

00:52:30.892 --> 00:52:33.231
But yeah, that's what I
love about tsukemen though,

